<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">States Reorganisation Act 1956</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The States Reorganisation Act 1956 was a law of the Indian Parliament that redrew the boundaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s states largely along linguistic lines, following the report of the States Reorganisation Commission. From 1 November 1956 it abolished the distinction between Part A, B and C states, creating 14 states and six union territories, among them enlarged states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/states-reorganisation-act-1956.jpg" alt="States Reorganisation Act 1956" width="320" height="510" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of India · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_India.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> became independent on 15 August 1947, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> were encouraged to accede, and between 1947 and about 1950 their territories were integrated into the Indian Union: some were merged into provinces, others grouped into unions such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a> and Madhya Bharat. The Constitution that came into force on 26 January 1950 sorted the states into Part A, the nine former governors' provinces, Part B, eight former princely states or unions under a rajpramukh, Part C, ten states under chief commissioners, and Part D, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andaman-and-nicobar-islands">Andaman and Nicobar Islands</a>.</p><p>Demands for linguistic states were older: the first movement began in 1886 in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>, and Orissa Province was formed on a linguistic basis in 1936 through the efforts of Madhusudan Das. The Linguistic Provinces Commission of June 1948 rejected language as a basis for states, but in 1952 Potti Sreeramulu died after a 56-day hunger strike for a Telugu-speaking state, and the unrest that followed led to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-state">Andhra State</a> in 1953. In December 1953 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> appointed the States Reorganisation Commission under the retired Chief Justice Fazal Ali, with H. N. Kunzru and K. M. Panikkar; it reported on 30 September 1955.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The Act was enacted on 31 August 1956 and took effect together with the Constitution (Seventh Amendment) Act, under which the former Part A and B states became simply states and the Part C and D territories not merged into states became union territories. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-state">Andhra State</a> with the Telugu-speaking areas of Hyderabad State. Bombay State gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a>, Kutch and the Marathi-speaking districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangabad">Aurangabad</a>, while its southernmost districts went to Mysore State, which also received Coorg and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> speaking districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> and Hyderabad.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> joined Travancore-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> with Malabar district and Kasaragod taluk, while Kanyakumari district went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madras-state">Madras State</a>. Madhya Pradesh absorbed Madhya Bharat, Vindhya Pradesh and Bhopal State, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> took in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>. A separate act of the same day moved Purulia and Islampur from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>. The six union territories were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andaman-and-nicobar-islands">Andaman and Nicobar Islands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, Manipur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripura">Tripura</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himachal-pradesh">Himachal Pradesh</a>, and the Laccadive, Minicoy and Amindivi Islands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s state boundaries have changed since, through laws such as the Punjab Reorganisation Act of 1966, the reorganisations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in 2000 and the Andhra Pradesh Reorganisation Act of 2014. The Act of 1956 nonetheless remains the most extensive change in state boundaries since independence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “States Reorganisation Act, 1956” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>States Reorganisation Act</b> of 1956 completed the process: from 1 November 1956 Abu and Delwara, the Part C state of Ajmer, formerly the British province of Ajmer-Merwara, and the Sunel Tappa enclave were added, the Sironj subdivision went to Madhya Pradesh, and the office of Rajpramukh was…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>States Reorganisation Act</b> PEPSU was merged into Punjab on 1 November 1956.</blockquote><a href="/state/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>States Reorganisation Act</b> of 1956 the Tamil-speaking southern taluks went to Madras State, and Malabar was added from Madras, forming the new state of Kerala on 1 November 1956.</blockquote><a href="/state/travancore-cochin">Travancore-Cochin</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>States Reorganisation Act</b> Vindhya Pradesh was merged into Madhya Pradesh on 1 November 1956.</blockquote><a href="/state/vindhya-pradesh">Vindhya Pradesh</a></li></ul></section>
